WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.350
Instructor: I wanna start this section on

00:01.350 --> 00:03.270
rebasing by telling you a little bit

00:03.270 --> 00:06.570
about my own personal history with rebasing.

00:06.570 --> 00:07.620
It's a command.

00:07.620 --> 00:09.120
Git rebase is the command.

00:09.120 --> 00:13.230
That, there's a bit of a divide over in the Git community.

00:13.230 --> 00:15.750
Some people use this command all the time.

00:15.750 --> 00:17.700
Some companies ask that their developers

00:17.700 --> 00:19.170
use rebase all the time.

00:19.170 --> 00:21.510
Others avoid it like the plague.

00:21.510 --> 00:24.990
When I first started, when I first learned Git,

00:24.990 --> 00:27.600
I was actually told by the person I was learning

00:27.600 --> 00:30.787
from to try to avoid rebasing ever.

00:30.787 --> 00:32.160
"Don't worry about it.

00:32.160 --> 00:33.780
It can really screw things up for you

00:33.780 --> 00:35.400
and for other people on your team.

00:35.400 --> 00:36.810
It's not for beginners."

00:36.810 --> 00:38.337
And some of that is true for sure,

00:38.337 --> 00:43.260
but it was kind of, I don't know, a scary command for me,

00:43.260 --> 00:46.560
and I avoided it for quite a long time, for many years.

00:46.560 --> 00:48.300
And I got by just fine with Git.

00:48.300 --> 00:50.190
I didn't really run into many issues.

00:50.190 --> 00:52.230
Maybe there was once or twice I had to use it

00:52.230 --> 00:53.760
but I would just Google, you know,

00:53.760 --> 00:57.150
what I needed to do and find something on Stack Overflow.

00:57.150 --> 01:00.090
It just wasn't something I needed to do frequently.

01:00.090 --> 01:03.330
Anyway, one day I was teaching a bootcamp a couple years ago

01:03.330 --> 01:05.520
and a student asked me about rebase in front

01:05.520 --> 01:07.890
of the rest of the class and I was not all

01:07.890 --> 01:10.410
that prepared to give him a good response.

01:10.410 --> 01:13.260
So I went home, did more research, came back,

01:13.260 --> 01:14.700
gave a the lecture the next day.

01:14.700 --> 01:15.960
Anyway, doesn't really matter.

01:15.960 --> 01:19.410
But long story short, I use rebase all the time now.

01:19.410 --> 01:21.210
It can be very useful

01:21.210 --> 01:23.520
but you have to know when not to use it.

01:23.520 --> 01:25.440
There's a golden rule of rebasing

01:25.440 --> 01:28.020
and we will focus on that in this section.

01:28.020 --> 01:30.810
So there's a divide within the community

01:30.810 --> 01:34.890
because rebasing is an alternative to merging.

01:34.890 --> 01:36.360
There's two different things really

01:36.360 --> 01:38.010
that people do with rebasing.

01:38.010 --> 01:41.010
The first one is you can use Git rebase instead

01:41.010 --> 01:42.210
of Git merge.

01:42.210 --> 01:45.360
So some people like to do that and they'll use Git rebase

01:45.360 --> 01:47.280
or some companies will ask their developers

01:47.280 --> 01:49.770
to use Git rebase instead of merging.

01:49.770 --> 01:51.720
But other people get by just fine

01:51.720 --> 01:54.360
with Git merge and they avoid Git rebase.

01:54.360 --> 01:58.170
So there's two big workflows for combining branches.

01:58.170 --> 02:00.270
One is rebasing and one is merging.

02:00.270 --> 02:03.150
We'll talk about that starting in the next video

02:03.150 --> 02:06.240
and then the second use case for rebase is

02:06.240 --> 02:10.380
to clean up your own commits, clean up your Git history.

02:10.380 --> 02:12.660
We'll talk about that too towards the end.

02:12.660 --> 02:14.550
All right, so you can get by

02:14.550 --> 02:17.370
with just merging things as we have been doing.

02:17.370 --> 02:19.170
Rebase is not, in my opinion,

02:19.170 --> 02:22.434
one of the essential commands that you just have to know.

02:22.434 --> 02:24.960
As I said, I used Git for quite a while

02:24.960 --> 02:27.450
without really rebasing all that much,

02:27.450 --> 02:29.518
but it's definitely worth knowing about

02:29.518 --> 02:32.460
and these days I think it's gaining in popularity

02:32.460 --> 02:35.220
or at least equal to the Git merge crowd.

02:35.220 --> 02:36.333
Okay, so let's begin.
